Expose a port of the active LangSmith sandbox on the web and return the URL that reaches it.

The service must listen on `0.0.0.0` at the specified port. It is served at the root of its own
LangSmith domain, so root-absolute URLs (`/assets/app.js`, `/@vite/client`), WebSockets, and hot
reload work with no base-path configuration.

Always configure custom servers to send an explicit UTF-8 charset in the `Content-Type` header
for text responses (for example, `Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8`) so emoji and other
non-ASCII characters render correctly. A `<meta charset="utf-8">` tag alone is not a substitute
for the response header.

For custom web UIs, follow the viewer's system light/dark preference with
`prefers-color-scheme` and `color-scheme: light dark` instead of forcing a theme.

The link carries no credential and never expires: whoever opens it signs in with their own
LangSmith session, and any member of this sandbox's LangSmith workspace gets through.

You cannot open the URL yourself: it is behind the LangSmith login, so the bot has no session for
it. Verify the service by browsing to the in-sandbox localhost with `agent-browser` instead, e.g.
`http://localhost:{{ port }}` from inside the sandbox — never through the shared service URL.

Every request reaches the service with the viewer's identity. Inbound copies of these headers are
stripped first, so a client cannot forge them:

- `X-Langsmith-User-Id` and `X-Langsmith-User-Email` (omitted when the account has no email):
  plain values. Use these by default, including to gate access.
- `X-Langsmith-User-Token`: an EdDSA-signed JWT minted per request and good for ten minutes. Use it
  only when there is a strong reason to, such as code that must also work outside this sandbox.
  Claims are `sub` (the LangSmith user id),
  `email`, `name`, `iss` (the LangSmith app URL) and `aud` (a one-element array holding the service
  host). Verify it with a JWT library against `{{ jwks_url }}` — the keys live on the LangSmith API
  host, not on the app URL the token names as its issuer.

The credential that authenticated the request never reaches the service.

Sharing one port twice returns the same link. A port that already handed out a short-lived
service token is refused until that token expires.
